WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.533
Spectral bisection splits a graph into two groups.

2
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.800
Both groups non-empty.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:17.267
And a declared balance window, here 2 to 3 a side; inside it, make the cut small.

4
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:21.400
Five nodes have 15 non-empty bipartitions.

5
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.600
Step one: build the Laplacian L = D − A.

6
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.289
Row A: degree 5 + 1 = 6, −5 to B, −1 to C.

7
00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:34.689
Every row sums to 0.

8
00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:42.089
Why L? Look at the quadratic form: each edge adds w times the squared difference of its ends.

9
00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:46.689
The heavier the edge, the closer its two numbers must be.

10
00:00:46.689 --> 00:00:52.600
Among unit vectors orthogonal to all-ones, the minimum is λ₂.

11
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.178
The vector that attains it is the Fiedler vector.

12
00:00:57.578 --> 00:01:03.267
G01's Fiedler vector gives each node a number.

13
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:06.956
The order: E, D, C, B, A.

14
00:01:06.956 --> 00:01:12.644
Tightly linked nodes sit together; C, linked to both sides, sits between.

15
00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:17.778
Along the order, try a cut between each pair of neighbours.

16
00:01:17.778 --> 00:01:21.689
The cuts are 6, 3, 5, 6.

17
00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:28.489
Only k = 2 and 3 are in the window: choose k = 2 with cut 3, not the middle.

18
00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:34.133
Two warnings. First, the sign: −x is an eigenvector too.

19
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.267
The order reverses; the same cut just swaps sides.

20
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:44.800
Second, when the graph is disconnected λ₂ = 0, and the order only reflects components.

21
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.333
Four things to keep.

22
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:50.600
Declare non-empty sides and a window;

23
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.178
build L, sort by Fiedler, sweep;

24
00:01:55.178 --> 00:02:00.711
Sign flips change nothing; check connectivity. Try it in the lab below.
